-### Reliable Reset Negotiated
+### RESET_STREAM_AT dialect negotiation
 
-TODO
+MsQuic can request and negotiate either or both of the modern
+RESET_STREAM_AT dialects:
+
+| Dialect | Public mask bit | Transport parameter | Frame type |
+|---------|-----------------|---------------------|------------|
+| draft-07 | `QUIC_RESET_STREAM_AT_DIALECT_DRAFT_07` (`1u << 0`) | `0x17f7586d2cb571` | `0x24` |
+| draft-10 | `QUIC_RESET_STREAM_AT_DIALECT_DRAFT_10` (`1u << 1`) | `0x1d` | `0x24` |
+
+Applications attest runtime support by querying the get-only
+`QUIC_PARAM_GLOBAL_RESET_STREAM_AT_DIALECTS` descriptor. Version 1 reports
+the supported mask and the exact transport-parameter and frame identities.
+Applications then set `QUIC_PARAM_CONFIGURATION_RESET_STREAM_AT_DIALECT_MASK`
+to zero, either dialect bit, or both bits. An explicit value, including zero,
+overrides the deprecated `QUIC_SETTINGS.ReliableResetEnabled` alias. Setting
+that Boolean to `TRUE` requests all supported modern dialects; setting it to
+`FALSE` requests none.
+
+Each endpoint advertises exactly its requested dialects. Because the transport
+parameter advertises support for receiving `RESET_STREAM_AT`, frame permission
+is directional: a nonzero peer mask permits local sending, while a nonzero
+local requested mask permits receiving from the peer. The two modern dialects
+share frame type `0x24`, so these permissions remain valid when the endpoints
+advertise different modern transport-parameter identities.
+
+For reporting, the negotiated mask is the intersection of the local requested
+mask and the exact dialects present in the peer's live-handshake transport
+parameters. When the local requested mask is nonzero,
+`QUIC_CONNECTION_EVENT_RELIABLE_RESET_NEGOTIATED` is delivered exactly once
+before `QUIC_CONNECTION_EVENT_CONNECTED`, whether or not the intersection is
+empty. Its payload reports the requested, peer, and negotiated masks, and
+`IsNegotiated` is equivalent to a nonzero negotiated mask. The intersection is
+common-dialect metadata; it is not the directional frame-permission check.
+A peer receive-capability mask restored from a resumption ticket permits 0-RTT
+RESET_STREAM_AT sending. It does not produce the public negotiation event or
+its reported intersection; those remain tied to the live handshake transport
+parameters, which replace the remembered peer mask.
+
+The stock MsQuic 2.6.0 legacy dialect (transport parameter
+`0x17f7586d2cb570`, frame `0x21`) is deliberately neither advertised nor
+recognized as RESET_STREAM_AT support. This preview wave negotiates the modern
+wire identities and gates use of the `0x24` frame. The first reset commits an
+immutable Final Size and application error code; later reset frames may only
+reduce Reliable Size. Final Size is checked against stream and connection flow
+control, and senders defer the reset frame until that credit is available.
+Receivers continue delivering through the smallest Reliable Size before
+reporting the abrupt reset.
 
